# What never enters the repository.
#
# There is one rule: only what the machine produces from things that are already
# versioned goes here. An intermediate artefact freezes the moment it is committed --
# `openapi.json` is the sharpest case, because it looks like a contract and is a dump
# taken before the last edit of the schemas. Contracts that ARE to be committed
# (`frontend/src/api/schema.d.ts`) are deliberately not listed here: CI regenerates
# and compares them, so a divergence is detected rather than hidden.
#
# The real HTTP contract lives in `contracts/openapi/` and is WRITTEN BY HAND.
# That settles an ambiguity the paragraph above carried from the start: "looks like a
# contract" meant there was no contract at all. `openapi.json` stays on this side of
# the line -- it is the dump `./scripts/contracts.sh` compares the authored contract
# against -- and a file under `contracts/` on the other, and the `contracts` gate refuses one that
# carries a generator's banner.
#
# This file's twin for the image is `.dockerignore`, and it is NOT a copy of it --
# that one also excludes the documentation and the tests a runtime image does not need.

# The Python environment and the traces of a run
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*

# Local databases. `--sqlite` starts an empty one in the repository root, and the
# OpenAPI dump makes its own so as not to touch that one.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
openapi-dump.db

# Build artefacts
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
app/static/
openapi.json

# JUnit reports from e2e. They can carry the content of requests and responses, so they
# are not merely an artefact -- they are an artefact that must not be committed (the
# constitution, art. XI).
e2e/reports/

# Working artefacts of the SDD process and of runs: application logs, coverage,
# screenshots from failed smokes, locks. Per machine, not per repository -- and a
# screenshot of a full data view is exactly the same art. XI.
.sdd/

# Locks on the process's state files. They exist for the length of one write and are
# gone; a committed lock looks to the next session like a change record somebody is
# holding and nobody is. `uv.lock` and `package-lock.json` are NOT this -- those are
# dependency lockfiles and belong in the repository.
*.lock
!uv.lock
!package-lock.json

# The operating system and editors
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Terraform -- local artefacts, never in the repository.
#
# `.terraform/` is downloaded providers (hundreds of megabytes) and the module cache.
# The state files are not here because they are large but because they **carry
# secrets**: the database master's password, ARNs, the whole configuration. The
# environments' state lives in S3 (`envs/*/backend.tf`); the only local state is the
# bootstrap, which creates that bucket and by definition cannot use it.
#
# `.terraform.lock.hcl` is an EXCEPTION and is to be committed: it is a dependency
# lockfile, just like `uv.lock` -- it pins provider versions so that two machines plan
# the same thing.
infra/**/.terraform/
infra/**/*.tfstate
infra/**/*.tfstate.*
infra/**/*.tfplan
infra/**/*.tfvars.local
!infra/**/.terraform.lock.hcl

# The mock-up export from Claude Design, as the browser hands it over. The content
# `spec/design/conventions.md` § Language stands on is unpacked and TRACKED in
# `spec/rationale/mockup-guestbook/` -- the archive is only a wrapper, and two copies of
# the same material drift exactly the way any other two copies do.
*.zip

